    "rawResponseText": "A **hotel franchise agreement** lets the owner operate the hotel under a brand’s name and system while keeping more day-to-day control, whereas a **management agreement** gives the brand or operator authority to run the hotel on the owner’s behalf.[1][2][5]\n\nThe practical difference is **control**: in a franchise, the owner is the operator and pays fees for brand access; in a management agreement, the owner hires the brand/operator to manage staffing, operations, and performance for a fee.[1][2][5]\n\n| Aspect | Franchise agreement | Management agreement |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Who runs the hotel** | The owner/franchisee runs it, often with its own staff or a third-party operator.[1][5] | The brand or management company runs it for the owner.[2][5] |\n| **Control** | More owner control over daily operations, but must follow brand standards.[1][5][6] | Less owner control; operator has more authority over operations.[1][2][5] |\n| **Fees** | Franchise fees, royalties, marketing/distribution/IT fees.[2][5] | Management fees, often base plus incentive fees, plus similar brand-related charges.[2][3] |\n| **Risk** | Owner bears operating risk while keeping control.[2][5] | Owner still bears property risk, but the operator handles management risk.[2][5] |\n| **Complexity** | Usually simpler and less negotiated than management agreements.[1] | Typically longer, more complex, and heavily negotiated.[1][2] |\n| **Best for** | Owners who want brand power but operational autonomy.[2][5] | Owners who want a more hands-off model and professional hotel management.[2][4] |\n\nA useful rule of thumb is: choose a **franchise** if you want to keep operating control and hire your own team, and choose **management** if you want the brand to run the hotel for you.[1][2][4]\n\nIf you want, I can also give you a **pros/cons list** or explain which model is usually better for **financing, control, or profitability**.",
